1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:00,916
qwengl.

2
00:00:00,916 --> 00:00:05,483
A GL style graphics library that runs inside a language model.

3
00:00:05,483 --> 00:00:07,767
No shaders, no rasteriser code.

4
00:00:07,767 --> 00:00:10,567
The model's own tokens are the pixels.

5
00:00:11,570 --> 00:00:17,126
The qwenTPU project compiles a tiny integer compute array into the weights of Qwen three point

6
00:00:17,126 --> 00:00:17,421
five.

7
00:00:17,421 --> 00:00:21,736
Every stage is a five bit network: a matrix multiply, a relu, and a clip.

8
00:00:21,736 --> 00:00:26,937
We wrote the layer above it: a compiler from a GL style API into programs for that chip.

9
00:00:26,937 --> 00:00:32,375
The weights ride in the prompt, the model emits every output token, and the next stage reads

10
00:00:32,375 --> 00:00:33,143
those tokens.

11
00:00:34,140 --> 00:00:35,405
The API looks like GL.

12
00:00:35,405 --> 00:00:40,579
Set a light, clear the color and depth buffers, draw triangle meshes with a rotation and a

13
00:00:40,579 --> 00:00:43,971
translation, and read back the pixels and the depth buffer.

14
00:00:43,971 --> 00:00:48,800
Swap the numpy backend for the Qwen backend, and every kernel runs inside the model.

15
00:00:49,800 --> 00:00:52,480
A draw call becomes a schedule of small kernels.

16
00:00:52,480 --> 00:00:57,784
Vertices are transformed, edge functions are evaluated without a multiplier, coverage is a hard

17
00:00:57,784 --> 00:01:03,087
threshold, the depth test is four relu layers, and flat shading gives five brightness levels in

18
00:01:03,087 --> 00:01:03,590
six hues.

19
00:01:03,590 --> 00:01:08,614
The framebuffer and depth buffer are tokens that the host holds between kernels, and every

20
00:01:08,614 --> 00:01:10,847
value in them was produced by the model.

21
00:01:11,850 --> 00:01:12,537
The result.

22
00:01:12,537 --> 00:01:18,406
Across three scenes and 120 frames, with 154.4 tokens through Qwen three point five zero point

23
00:01:18,406 --> 00:01:23,338
eight B on an A100, the model's argmax matched the integer oracle 0 mismatches.

24
00:01:23,338 --> 00:01:27,708
Every frame is identical to the numpy pipeline, in color and in depth.

25
00:01:28,710 --> 00:01:36,218
The dungeon is the largest run: sixty by sixty pixels, seven draw calls per frame, forty eight

26
00:01:36,218 --> 00:01:36,777
frames.

27
00:01:36,777 --> 00:01:42,368
That is 812,548 kernel runs and 84.4 tokens, in about 37 of A100 time.

28
00:01:43,370 --> 00:01:47,919
Frame zero is the slowest because the static room has to be computed once.

29
00:01:47,919 --> 00:01:52,038
After that it is cached, and each frame only recomputes what moved.

30
00:01:53,040 --> 00:01:59,427
The big runs are teacher forced, so one frame of each scene was also run free running, where

31
00:01:59,427 --> 00:02:01,995
the host only does argmax and append.

32
00:02:01,995 --> 00:02:08,451
The dungeon frame took 17,217 kernel runs and 8,235,862 tokens, and all 3 free running frames

33
00:02:08,451 --> 00:02:11,645
were identical to numpy, with zero mismatches.

34
00:02:12,640 --> 00:02:14,157
Now what is not in the model.

35
00:02:14,157 --> 00:02:18,708
The host computes the per draw uniforms, bins triangles and moves data between kernels.

36
00:02:18,708 --> 00:02:23,416
The projection is orthographic, depth is flat per triangle, shading is flat, and oversized

37
00:02:23,416 --> 00:02:25,613
triangles are dropped rather than clipped.

38
00:02:25,613 --> 00:02:30,426
And the long runs are teacher forced, with one frame of each scene run free running to close

39
00:02:30,426 --> 00:02:30,844
the gap.

40
00:02:31,840 --> 00:02:35,866
The code, the run logs and every animation are in the repo.

